# Node admission · `rec:node:scope`

**Definition (Scope)** · `def:node:scope` The library admits the reader closure of (`[ROOT-proposal:api:dependencies]`) and the unserved artifact-agreement scope of (`[ROOT-amend:api:test-schema-plan]`). It contains no executable, operation executor, listener, store or served configuration. Dependency admission establishes no execution bound. A reader that accepts structural disagreement or a substituted input can admit a different edition from the reviewed source. The finite reader controls (`claim:admission:control-schema-register-agree`), (`claim:admission:schema-reader-refuses-invalid-shapes`), (`claim:admission:register-syntax-refuses`), (`claim:admission:register-unknown-keys-refuse`), (`claim:admission:register-metadata-is-closed`), (`claim:admission:pending-register-cannot-serve`), (`claim:admission:embedding-is-build-owned`), (`claim:admission:embedded-child-has-no-policy-path`) support (`verif:node:admission`).

**Verification (Unserved reader admission)** · `verif:node:admission` The test-only admission harness parses the build-owned schema through the admitted carriage and SDL reader and the independent TOML register draft through its closed reader. It compares roots, fields, result types and nullability; checks closed operation metadata, failure names, applicability and pending measurements; and retains only first-party refusal classes. The artifact and draft are embedded and compared byte for byte with their reviewed inputs, including the record’s draft; substituted runtime files are not read. No executor or served configuration is constructed, including for synthetic admitted-limit fixtures. This establishes reader agreement only, not execution bounds, scalar transport or cancellation. Its finite conformance and robustness controls are (`claim:admission:control-schema-register-agree`), (`claim:admission:schema-reader-refuses-invalid-shapes`), (`claim:admission:register-syntax-refuses`), (`claim:admission:register-unknown-keys-refuse`), (`claim:admission:register-metadata-is-closed`), (`claim:admission:pending-register-cannot-serve`), (`claim:admission:embedding-is-build-owned`), (`claim:admission:embedded-child-has-no-policy-path`).

The designed pn executable has explicit `start` and `edition` commands under (`[ROOT-amend:api:health-lifecycle]`); neither accepts an edition argument. Exactly one of `PN_EDITION` and the public edition-only file named by `PN_EDITION_FILE` supplies selection, and both supplied or neither supplied refuses as usage, with no default or precedence. The `edition` command reports the edition, source and embedded-roster support as one JSON object without dispatching start; the roster contains only pairs with both generated artifacts embedded at their assigned coordinates, so the first pair reports supported=false before its register is generated. Supported is not a promise that pn start will start. As a result command it refuses terminal stdout with usage exit 2 and must be piped or redirected; its help states this. The shell refuses both descriptor advertisements and the public PN_CONNECTION_INPUT directory-delivery marker before acquiring any connection input or directory path; C9 must decide and admit the eventual delivery form. pn start requires the supervisor’s notification socket and cannot be started by hand outside such a supervisor; its help states this. Readiness uses that local notification protocol, and only SIGTERM is graceful termination. These are design contracts, not implemented command surfaces; internal lifecycle transitions and process binding remain their later subcuts.
